# Code owners — GitHub auto-requests a review from the owner when a
# matching path changes. It is NOT a merge gate here, by ruling:
# "Require review from Code Owners" would deadlock every owner PR to
# an owned path on a solo repo with enforce_admins on, so
# scripts/protect-main.sh keeps it off and ties the flip to a second
# Write collaborator, together with the approval count (#1521). What
# stops an outsider changing an owned path is that only the owner
# can merge; this file makes sure the owner is asked to look.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so keep the most specific ones last.

# Website + published docs — content a PR could quietly alter.
/site/            @hajiboy95
/docs/            @hajiboy95

# Build + CI + this file itself — highest-trust surfaces.
/.github/         @hajiboy95
/scripts/         @hajiboy95

# The licence chain — what a contribution is licensed under and
# what a contributor agrees to; a reword lands only through the
# owner's merge, and this asks the owner to read it (#1521).
/LICENSE                  @hajiboy95
/CLA.md                   @hajiboy95
/CONTRIBUTING.md          @hajiboy95

# Dependencies / lockfile — the sneakiest supply-chain vector; a
# lockfile bump is easy to rubber-stamp, so pin an explicit owner.
/site/package.json        @hajiboy95
/site/package-lock.json   @hajiboy95
